18:29:53.624769 - BRAILLE LINE:  'Bloomberg 取消訂閱 上午5:35 (12 小時前) 未加星號 無法使用表情符號回應群組 回覆 更多郵件選項 寄給 我 顯示詳細資料 這封郵件似乎是以英文撰寫而成 翻譯成中文（繁體） 關閉 Read in browser Bloomberg Asia Good morning. The yen is on the move as a BOJ hawk revives rate-hike bets. India is building its defenses with an eye on a $20 trillion economy. And Bali is turning to an electric tram to tackle its infamous traffic. Listen to the day’s top stories . — Hari Govind Market Snapshot Yen ¥158.68 -0.0% S&P 500 7,666.60 +0.5% Market data as of 05:24 PM ET. Data is subject to provider delays. Your watchlist The yen strengthened sharply against the dollar, partly driven by hawkish comments from Bank of Japan board member Hajime Takata, who left the door open to a larger interest-rate hike and back-to-back increases. The move put traders on alert for signs that authorities may also be stepping in to support the currency, though strategists said it was smaller than past swings linked to intervention. Image associated with story "Bessent Leaves BOJ in No-Win Situation Over Japan Rate Hikes" Bessent Leaves BOJ in No-Win Situation Over Japan Rate Hikes Read more China won’t have any debt claims on revenue from Venezuela’s new oil production , US Energy Secretary Chris Wright told Bloomberg Television in Caracas. Wright joined executives from Chevron, GE Vernova and Eni, along with acting President Delcy Rodríguez, to unveil a wave of energy deals aimed at boosting the country’s crude output. Uber Technologies is cutting 10% of its global workforce, or about 3,300 jobs, as part of a major restructuring to trim management layers and focus spending on ride-sharing, delivery and robotaxis. The company is also moving closer to a major acquisition, with Delivery Hero’s board voting to accept its nearly $15 billion takeover offer. Broadcom gave a forecast that missed estimates, a sign the chipmaker is seeing a slower payoff from the AI boom than some expected. The industry is also facing a potential policy shake-up, with the Trump administration preparing broader tariffs on semiconductor imports and relief for companies that make chips in the US, Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick told CNBC. Berkshire Hathaway CEO Greg Abel pledged long-term support for five Japanese trading houses after the conglomerate raised its stake in each to more than 10%. Speaking from Japan on CNBC Wednesday, Abel said Berkshire expects the firms’ earnings to keep growing, with potential increases in dividends and share buybacks. Claim over 50% off before summer ends As Q4 approaches, the rest of your year comes into focus. Prepare for whatever comes next with a Bloomberg.com subscription. Get your first year for just $399 $170. Offer expires September 11, 2026 11:59 PM EST. Unlock more than 50% off Deep Dive: Building Buffers Modi during the Shanghai Cooperation Organisation summit in Bishkek, Kyrgyzstan. India is building its defenses — and not just for the rupee. From tapping its vast diaspora to climate-proofing prime real estate, the country is erecting buffers as it sets its sights on a $20 trillion economy. • The economy will probably approach that figure by 2046 if it keeps growing at its current pace, according to Chief Economic Adviser V. Anantha Nageswaran — putting Prime Minister Narendra Modi’s developed-nation goal within reach . • India also recently pulled in $136 billion in foreign currency, blowing past the central bank’s $80 billion estimate . Most came from diaspora deposits, giving policymakers a more effective weapon to defend the rupee when markets turn rough. • New Delhi is looking to open more doors for trade, too . India plans to seal deals with Israel and the six-nation Gulf Cooperation Council once regional tensions ease, part of a push to diversify its export markets. • And investors are preparing for a different kind of threat: extreme weather. Brookfield spent $26 million flood-proofing a once-struggling Mumbai office park, helping take occupancy from 16% to more than 99%. • That’s part of a bigger shift across India’s $300 billion real estate market. As floods and extreme rainfall become more frequent, investors are spending on drainage, barriers and even roads — turning climate resilience into a new measure of what a property is worth. The Big Take Image associated with story "A Data Center Revolt By Historic Battlefield Is Now a Blueprint for Backlash" A Data Center Revolt By Historic Battlefield Is Now a Blueprint for Backlash Organized opposition toppled a $100 billion project from developers backed by two of the world’s biggest private equity companies.  Read more Image associated with story "Big Take Asia: Nepal’s Floods Expose Hydropower Risks (Podcast)" Nepal’s Floods Expose Hydropower Risks (Podcast) Opinion The White House’s strategy on the long-frozen conflict on the Korean Peninsula is moving in the wrong direction , Bloomberg’s editors write. To help keep Kim Jong Un in check, the US, South Korea and Japan should be knitting together their missile defenses more seamlessly, perhaps even discussing a three-way mutual-defense pledge. More Opinions Image associated with story "This Global Bond Rout Has a Surprise Winner" Shuli Ren This Global Bond Rout Has a Surprise Winner Image associated with story "We’re Going About Nuclear Shipping the Wrong Way" David Fickling We’re Going About Nuclear Shipping the Wrong Way Play Alphadots! Our daily word puzzle with a plot twist. Alphadots: The Word Puzzle With a Plot Twist by Bloomberg Today’s clue is: Defense contractor? Play now! Before You Go Canggu, Bali. Bali has a new plan to tackle its notorious traffic: an electric tram. The 12-kilometer (7.5-mile) line will connect the island’s international airport with tourist hotspot Canggu, running along the western coast. Construction is set to begin in March 2027, with service scheduled to start in 2029. A Few More Image associated with story "Can You Air-Condition a City?" Can You Air-Condition a City? Image associated with story "Tiger Wood